<Site title="Same season, different year"><PageHeader title="Same season, different year" eyebrow="Historical context" description="The useful question is which earlier seasons can be compared with the same place, dates and observing method." /><section class="section"><div class="shell page-grid"><article><h2>What makes a comparison eligible?</h2><p>Match the region, season cutoff, unit, observation series and normal period. A full June–September historical total cannot be ranked against a current June–August value. A documentary description from the nineteenth century belongs in a separate evidence layer from a measured series.</p><div class="figure"><p class="eyebrow">Comparator status</p><h3>No current rank is published</h3><p>A matched 2026 cutoff and independently replayed historical series are required before a rank or frequency can be calculated.</p><p class="small muted">There is no invented line, historical band or “worst ever” label here.</p></div><h2>Why rainfall alone is insufficient</h2><p>Earlier water outcomes were shaped by storage, irrigation, access, markets, institutions and public health. An analogue should explain where those systems differ, rather than turning a rain total into a forecast of consequences.</p><h2>Sources under review</h2><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://www.imdpune.gov.in/cmpg/Griddata/Rainfall_25_NetCDF.html">IMD Pune historical gridded rainfall</a><small>Series version, geographic footprint and reuse terms need review.</small></li><li><a href="https://censusindia.gov.in/nada/index.php/catalog/6191">Census of India 2011 Primary Census Abstract</a><small>2011 counts are a dated census, not a 2026 population measurement.</small></li></ul></article><aside><p class="eyebrow">Continue</p><a href={url('/water-and-people/')}>Population and demand</a><a href={url('/methodology/')}>Comparison rules</a><a href={url('/data-status/')}>What is missing</a></aside></div></section></Site>
